% The fact set the WORKING and HELD-OUT eval banks are scored against.
%
% `facts.dl` next door is the M1 fixture and is frozen with the M1 bank. It no
% longer loads: `exported` and `implements` are asserted there as facts, and
% both became RULE heads in schema 2/3, which safety rule 8 refuses. That file
% is a record of what M1 measured, so it is left exactly as it was and this one
% is written against the vocabulary that ships today.
%
% Nothing here comes from an extractor. Every row is a row a real index would
% hold, written by hand so the banks test the schema and the rule layer rather
% than the extractors. It is deliberately richer than `facts.dl`, because the
% frozen 30 leave most of `rules/stdlib.dl` untouched. What it adds, and why:
%
%   * `visibility` + `name_export` instead of asserted `exported`, so all three
%     `exported` clauses fire — including the recursive `inherited` one.
%   * `scip_impl` + `name_impl`, so `implements` has both provenances and the
%     `trait_sym` and `S != T` guards each have a row they refuse.
%   * a Rust file the indexer never saw (`src/legacy.rs`), which is what makes
%     the `_exact` twins DIVERGE rather than agree.
%   * `module` definitions, so `calls_at`'s `FromKind != "module"` guard is
%     exercised instead of advertised.
%   * a tier-B opaque placeholder (`local web/app.ts 7`), so `scip_claim`'s
%     "emitted but not resolved" case is real.
%   * four `Role` values, one of them `test`, so every `is_test` clause has a
%     file and the role vocabulary is not all `unknown`.
%   * a layered `src/` tree with one real layering violation and one policy
%     that holds, so the conformance shape of `docs/cookbook.md` § 1 has both
%     a question that answers and a question that correctly answers nothing.
%
% Tiers, by language:
%   rust       SCIP, except `src/legacy.rs` which the indexer skipped
%   typescript SCIP, with one unresolved placeholder occurrence
%   go         tree-sitter only
%   python     tree-sitter only

